Types of Operating Systems — With Real-Time Examples 
Type of OS	Simple Meaning	Real-Time Example
1. Batch Operating System	Executes jobs in groups without continuous user interaction.	

Payroll processing — thousands of employee salaries are processed together.

2. Time-Sharing Operating System	A time-sharing OS gives each process/user a small time slice of the CPU.

After that small time expires, the OS switches the CPU to another process. Because switching happens very quickly, everything appears to run at the same time.
 
Simple example

Suppose you have one CPU and 3 processes:

CPU
 ↓
Process A → 10 ms
Process B → 10 ms
Process C → 10 ms
Process A → 10 ms
Process B → 10 ms
...

The OS keeps switching between them.

Real-life example

You are using:

Chrome
YouTube/music
MS Word

You can type in Word while music is playing and Chrome is open because the OS repeatedly gives each process CPU time.

3. Multiprogramming OS: A multiprogramming operating system keeps multiple programs in main memory at the same time and switches the CPU to another program when the current 
program is waiting for an I/O operation.

The main goal is to keep the CPU busy and avoid wasting CPU time.

Simple example

Suppose 3 programs are in memory:

Main Memory
┌──────────────┐
│ Program A    │
│ Program B    │
│ Program C    │
└──────────────┘

CPU starts executing Program A.

If Program A needs to read a file from the disk, the CPU would otherwise sit idle.

Instead, the OS gives the CPU to Program B:

CPU → Program A
       ↓
    Waiting for I/O

CPU → Program B
       ↓
    Executes

When B waits for I/O, the CPU can run Program C.

So the CPU stays busy.

Example 1

You have:

Program A: Reading data from disk
Program B: Performing calculations
Program C: Printing data

While A is waiting for disk I/O, the CPU executes B.

4. Multitasking OS	: A multitasking operating system allows the user to run multiple programs/processes seemingly at the same time by rapidly switching the CPU between them.
Each task gets a small amount of CPU time, not a separate CPU.

For example, you have one CPU core:

CPU
 ↓
Chrome → Word → Music → Chrome → Word → Music

The OS gives each process a small amount of CPU time and switches between them very quickly. This is called context switching.

Example 1: Normal computer use

You are:

Watching a YouTube video
Downloading a file
Typing in MS Word

The OS manages all these processes so you can use them together.	

Windows — listening to music while using Chrome and Word.

5. MULTI PROCESSOR : A multiprocessing operating system allows a computer to use two or more CPUs/CPU cores to execute multiple processes at the same time.

Because multiple processes can run in parallel, the system can complete work faster and use the CPU efficiently.

Example

Suppose your computer has 4 CPU cores and you are doing these 4 tasks:

Process 1: Playing a video
Process 2: Downloading a file
Process 3: Running a Python program
Process 4: Listening to music

The OS can distribute these processes across different CPU cores:

CPU Core 1 → Video
CPU Core 2 → Download
CPU Core 3 → Python program
CPU Core 4 → Music

So the tasks can actually execute in parallel.

6. Real-Time OS (RTOS)	Responds to events within a strict time limit.

Car airbag system — detects a crash and deploys the airbag immediately.

7. Distributed OS	: A Distributed OS manages multiple computers connected through a network and makes them appear like one single system to the user.

Computer A ─┐
Computer B ─┼──→ Distributed OS → User sees one system
Computer C ─┘

Example 1: A distributed computing system where several computers share processing work.

8. Network OS	Manages computers and resources connected through a network.	

Company network — employees access shared files and printers from a server.

9. Mobile Operating System: A Mobile Operating System (Mobile OS) is an operating system designed specifically for mobile devices such as smartphones and tablets.

It manages the device's hardware, applications, memory, storage, network, touch screen, camera, sensors, and battery.

Example 1: Android 📱

Google's Android is a mobile OS used on phones from companies such as Samsung and OnePlus.

10. Embedded Operating System	: An Embedded Operating System (Embedded OS) is an operating system designed to run on a specific device or machine and perform a specific task or set of tasks.

Unlike a laptop OS, it is usually designed for limited hardware resources such as small memory, low processing power, and low storage.

Washing machine — controls washing, rinsing, spinning, and timing.
🔥 Easy examples to remember for exams
Batch OS → Salary processing
Time-Sharing OS → College computer lab
Multiprogramming → Download + run another program
Multitasking → Music + Chrome + Word
Multiprocessing → Multiple CPU cores
Real-Time OS → Car airbag
Distributed OS → Multiple computers working together
Network OS → Company server
Mobile OS → Android
Embedded OS → Washing machine

Disk Scheduling Algorithms

First: What is Disk Scheduling?
Definition:
Disk scheduling is the technique used by the Operating System to decide which disk I/O request should be served first.
The main purpose is to reduce disk-head movement and improve disk performance.

Real-time example
Imagine a lift (elevator) in a building.
People are waiting on floors 2, 5, 8, and 10. The lift must decide which floor to visit first.
Similarly, a disk has many requests, and the disk head must decide which request to handle first.

1. FCFS — First Come First Serve

Definition

Requests are processed in the exact order in which they arrive.

Easy to understand
Suppose requests arrive like this:
A → B → C → D
The disk serves:

A → B → C → D

It doesn't care which request is closer.

Real-time example

Bank queue:

People arrive:

Person A → Person B → Person C

The bank serves them in the same order.

Another example: Print queue — the first document sent to the printer is printed first.

Remember
FCFS = First request → First service

2. SSTF — Shortest Seek Time First

The disk serves the request that is closest to the current position of the disk head.

Easy to understand

Suppose the disk head is at 50.

Requests:
20, 45, 55, 90

Distances from 50:
20 → 30
45 → 5
55 → 5
90 → 40

So the disk chooses 45 or 55, because they are closest.

Real-time example

Think of an elevator at floor 50.

Requests are from floors:
20, 45, 55, 90

The elevator chooses a nearby floor first rather than going all the way to 90.

Another example: A delivery person at one location chooses the nearest delivery first.

Remember

SSTF = Serve the nearest request first

⚠️ Problem: A request that is far away may keep waiting for a long time. This is called starvation.

3. SCAN — Elevator Algorithm

The disk head moves in one direction, serving requests along the way. When it reaches the end, it changes direction.

That's why SCAN is also called the Elevator Algorithm.

Easy to understand

Suppose:

Head = 50
Requests = 20, 40, 60, 80

Head is moving right

It serves:

50 → 60 → 80 → end → 40 → 20

Then it comes back.

Real-time example

Think of an elevator.

It starts going upward:

Floor 5 → 6 → 8 → 10

Then it reaches the top and comes downward:
10 → 8 → 6 → 3

Another example: A security guard checks rooms from one end of a building to the other, then comes back.
Remember
SCAN = Go one way → reach end → come back

4. C-SCAN — Circular SCAN

The disk head moves in only one direction. When it reaches the end, it jumps back to the beginning and starts serving requests again.

Suppose requests are:

20, 40, 60, 80
Head = 50, moving right.

It goes:

50 → 60 → 80 → end → jump to beginning → 20 → 40

It does not serve requests while coming back.

Imagine a circular bus route.

The bus travels:
Stop 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5
Instead of turning around, it goes back to Stop 1 and starts the route again.
Another example: A round-robin inspection where a worker always moves in one direction and, after reaching the last location, returns to the first.
Remember
C-SCAN = Go one way → jump to beginning → continue

5. LOOK

LOOK works like SCAN, but the disk head does not travel all the way to the physical end of the disk.
It turns around when there are no more requests in that direction.

In the look it will not go to end in the scan 10 , 20 , 30 it will go to end then it will go to the position which is left of head but look will directly go to the left position of 
head instead of going to end 

Easy to understand

Suppose the disk has requests:

20, 40, 60, 80

Head = 50, moving right.

SCAN might go:

50 → 60 → 80 → 100 → reverse

But LOOK goes:

50 → 60 → 80 → reverse

It stops at 80 because there is no request after 80.

Real-time example

An elevator has requests on floors:

6, 8, 10

The elevator is going upward.

It doesn't need to go to the top floor if nobody requested it. It turns around after floor 10.

Another example: A security guard checks only the rooms that actually need inspection and turns around after the last requested room.
Remember
LOOK = SCAN, but don't go unnecessarily to the end

6. C-LOOK

C-LOOK works like C-SCAN, but the head doesn't travel to the physical end.

It goes only as far as the last request, then jumps to the first request.

Easy to understand
Requests:
20, 40, 60, 80
Head = 50, moving right.

C-LOOK:
50 → 60 → 80 → jump → 20 → 40
It doesn't go to the physical end.

Real-time example

Imagine a delivery person who has deliveries only at:
60, 80, 20, 40

They deliver in one direction:
60 → 80

Then instead of traveling through all the empty locations, they jump back to the first required location:
20 → 40

Another example: A circular inspection route that visits only locations where work is required.

Remember
C-LOOK = C-SCAN, but don't go to the end

